4
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:19.680
Headline inflation can miss what makes everyday life feel unaffordable.

5
00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:29.680
This discussion examines that gap through essentials, starting with buying a fridge and why delivering the basics matters for public trust.

6
00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:31.680
What is the point?

7
00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.600
Like we don't trust these folks.

8
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.280
They are like, you know, detached.

9
00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.440
They don't understand what my troubles are.

10
00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:45.600
I think that has become very clear as like at the core of the crisis of liberal democracy not delivering on literally the basics.

11
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:54.720
So on the question of the basics, I was thinking about this recently because I bought a new fridge in New York, the first fridge I've probably personally bought ever.

12
00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:55.840
And I just got a cheap one.

13
00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:08.400
And I was actually really surprised by how cheap it was compared to like I can either buy a fridge, which will hopefully be in my house for a few years, or I could pay for groceries for like a couple or a few weeks.

14
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:22.880
It seems like it seems like there's so much price deflation concentrated in appliances and nice things to have, but at the same time, the cost of actually living and eating and having shelter and consuming electricity has gone up enormously.

15
00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.320
How much does the actual distribution and price increases matter here?

16
00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:30.000
I think it's really core, and I think that's why the CPI is so misleading.

17
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.480
I mean, first of all, the CPI is what economists like to call a plutocratic index, right?

18
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:37.280
I mean measures every dollar in spending.

19
00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:44.240
So if you have $300,000 to spend a year, you count 10 times as much as someone who's $30,000 to spend.

20
00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:47.120
Um, and these spending patterns may be very different.

21
00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:56.160
So I don't know, I mean, fridges may be something that everybody needs, but you know, big luxury fridges with 15 different functions and so on might have deflated in price.

22
00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:04.360
But if at the same time rent went up and basic food prices went up in ways that just are pricing people out, then this is a crisis.

23
00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:09.000
I mean, we see, for example, electricity prices up 40%, right?

24
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.320
Where the incomes of um the bottom 20% have risen around 30%.

25
00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:26.680
And now if you are in a position where the bottom 50% are spending around 70% of their income on essentials, meaning, you know, housing, food, energy, the cost of running a car and healthcare.

26
00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:46.360
And if this is the scenario, and then one of these big ticket items goes up by 10%, then you, you know, immediately feel insecure because your buffer in your income that you can leverage, you know, paying more for that stuff is just so minuscule that um you are immediately, you know, with the back again.

27
00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:50.760
As AI becomes useful enough to run at home, how should we make it safe?

28
00:02:50.760 --> 00:03:00.840
This debate weighs incremental engineering against theoretical breakthroughs, including when warnings justify acting before anything goes wrong.

29
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:20.840
And if, for example, if the uh I I do see it as analogous to if you have a new technology, uh like a nuclear reactors or aircraft or whatever, and then you haven't actually seen anything go wrong, anything significant, but you have all these theoretical arguments for why things might go wrong, and you're introvening on the on the basis of that.

30
00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:25.640
I think that like I I'm just suspicious about that approach in in general.

31
00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:34.520
Like the thing I'm challenging when I say that is the view that like the way you get aligned AIs is to have some theoretical breakthrough in alignment.

32
00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:39.800
Like that's the thing I'm challenging, and what I would say is that like that's basically nothing in machine learning has worked like that.

33
00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:44.720
Like machine learning is not a field that has large theoretical breakthroughs.

34
00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:48.240
Like there's been like basically no large theoretical breakthroughs over like the past 10 years.

35
00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:49.680
All of them have been incremental.

36
00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:57.600
It's very incremental.

37
00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.240
Uh it's like there are a lot of dimensions that you optimize at once.

38
00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.240
And that's the way you make them more capable.

39
00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:08.160
So I don't see why you would assume anything different about like the way we would make them like more aligned.

40
00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:15.040
I think this is a key disagreement maybe between you guys is are there tractable interventions that we could make?

41
00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:25.600
And specifically, are those interventions more tractable if we get more time to make them that is likely to increase the value of what these AI systems do and how they treat us.

42
00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:35.440
Uh the AI Futures project has just put out a pacing scheme that you say will delay the intelligence explosion by a year, um, which I guess you are expecting in 2027.

43
00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.760
So you think this would slow down to 2028?

44
00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:38.240
Something like that.

45
00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:39.920
I mean, we have uncertainty, but yeah.

46
00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:43.600
What changes if we get the intelligence explosion in 2028 rather than 2027?

47
00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:44.720
Yeah, so a couple things.

48
00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:54.320
So, first of all, I think that um progress is being made on AI alignment and science of understanding how AI works and all sorts of things like this.

49
00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:56.560
It's not going fast enough, but it is being made.

50
00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:06.160
And so each additional year of time that we buy, there's additional progress, which um, you know, cuts away at that probability mass of the of the bad outcomes happening.

51
00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:24.080
This discussion examines how US industrial production diverged from GDP after China joined the WTO, and what that history means for today's reindustrialization investment case.

52
00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:25.200
GDP.

53
00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:32.920
But then those two growth rates started to diverge once China entered the World Trade Organization.

54
00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:37.320
Yeah, absolutely.

55
00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:36.760
So, sort of, can you explain sort of the what we're looking at here?

56
00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:44.920
So you're looking at one of my favorite charts and one of the the aha moments for me as I went through my as I went through my research, because you're exactly right.

57
00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:51.320
The chart that you're looking at shows US GDP and US industrial production indexed together.

58
00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:54.840
So you're looking at their growth rate, going all the way back to the 1920s.

59
00:05:54.840 --> 00:06:02.920
And what you can see when you look at those two series on the chart is that for the longest time, for decades and decades, those these two series were inherently linked.

60
00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:03.800
They were overlapped.

61
00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:05.640
As one went, so did the other.

62
00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:12.920
And the only thing that started to break that trend was right around the time that China entered the WTO, right around 2000.

63
00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:22.840
And when we think about what that means from a US perspective, is that for the longest time, US industrial growth and US GDP growth were tightly linked.

64
00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:25.640
And then over the last couple of decades, they haven't been.

65
00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:31.000
And the China answer is it kind of really starts to go a long way in understanding why.

66
00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:46.520
And the the thing that is interesting to me, and again, this is this is where you're pulling the threads and trying to understand this as you're as you're kind of researching it live, is that China's entry into the WTO really represented two important things from a global manual manufacturing perspective.

67
00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:52.680
The first is that China came to the WTTO with a genuine advantage, which is cheap labor, right?

68
00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:59.080
The Chinese population was one of the largest in the world, and the the citizens of China were moving from the farms to the cities.

69
00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:01.320
You had an enormous quantity of labor.

70
00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:05.960
And what we know from our standard economics is that when you have a lot of something, it tends to get cheaper.

71
00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:16.720
And so as a result, China kind of entered this WTO system where we were kind of, hey, you know, trade wherever it's the cheapest, labor arbitrage, things of that sort, starting with that huge advantage of low-cost labor.

72
00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:33.680
I think what many Western kind of economic studies missed, though, is that there was a second component as well, which is that China always viewed um industrial policy, the ability to make things for the world as not just an economic principle, but a geopolitical.

73
00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:00.400
Investors did the same thing again.

74
00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:02.400
They sold after decline.

75
